lessons.body:
An assertion states what must be true. In Playwright you write them with `expect`. The ones you should use for page content are **web-first assertions**: they take a locator or page, and they keep re-checking until the condition is true or a timeout (5 seconds by default) runs out.

```ts
await expect(page.getByText('Shift requested')).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Assigned to R. Rivera');
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/shifts\/\d+/);
await expect(page.getByRole('row')).toHaveCount(5);
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Assign' })).toBeEnabled();
```

Note the `await` in front of `expect`. Web-first assertions are asynchronous.

**Why retrying matters**

Real pages update after a delay: a request goes to the server, the list re-renders. If you check the text once, at the wrong instant, the test fails although the app works. A web-first assertion polls until the page catches up. That removes a whole class of false failures.

Compare:

```ts
// Weak: reads the value once, right now
const text = await page.getByRole('status').textContent();
expect(text).toBe('Assigned to R. Rivera');

// Strong: retries until true or timeout
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Assigned to R. Rivera');
```

The first can fail simply because the status has not updated yet. The second cannot, unless the status really never appears.

**Useful assertions**

| Assertion | Checks |
|---|---|
| `toBeVisible()` / `toBeHidden()` | Element shown or not |
| `toHaveText()` / `toContainText()` | Exact or partial text |
| `toHaveValue()` | Value of an input |
| `toHaveCount(n)` | Number of matches |
| `toHaveURL()` / `toHaveTitle()` | Page URL or title |
| `not.` prefix | Negation, also retried: `await expect(loc).not.toBeVisible()` |

**Make assertions mean something**

Assert the outcome a user cares about. "The Save button was clicked" is not an outcome. "The shift appears in the list with status Filled" is. A test that ends without any `expect` can only fail if something throws, so it proves little.

exercises.answer:
```ts
const row = page.getByTestId('shift-1042');
await expect(row).toContainText('Rivera');
await expect(row.getByText('Filled')).toBeVisible();
```

These are web-first assertions: they retry until the row updates or the timeout is reached. The list refreshes after a server response, so a single immediate check could fail even though the app is working.

lessons.body:
**The fixed sleep problem**

```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Assign' }).click();
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);   // avoid
await expect(page.getByText('Filled')).toBeVisible();
```

`waitForTimeout` pauses for a set time. Too short and the test fails on a slow machine; too long and the suite crawls. It also hides the real question: what are we waiting for? Playwright's documentation discourages it for tests. Waiting for the condition itself is both faster and safer:

```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Assign' }).click();
await expect(page.getByText('Filled')).toBeVisible();
```

If the app really is slow for a legitimate reason, raise the timeout for that one assertion instead:

```ts
await expect(page.getByText('Filled')).toBeVisible({ timeout: 15_000 });
```

**Waiting on the network**

Sometimes the right signal is a server response. You can wait for it directly:

```ts
const responsePromise = page.waitForResponse(
  r => r.url().includes('/api/assignments') && r.status() === 201
);
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Assign' }).click();
await responsePromise;
```

Start waiting before the click, so you cannot miss the response. Wait only for a request that really happens: on a site that changes pages inside the browser, no matching response may fire and the wait runs to the timeout (I hit this on playwright.dev). Use this when the UI gives no visible signal; prefer a visible assertion otherwise.

**Soft assertions**

A normal `expect` stops the test at the first failure. A soft assertion records the failure and continues, then fails the test at the end. It suits checking several independent facts on one page:

```ts
await expect.soft(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Shift 1042' })).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(page.getByText('Maple Street Pharmacy')).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(page.getByText('Filled')).toBeVisible();
```

You then see all three problems in one run. Do not use soft assertions where later steps depend on the earlier one passing.

**Polling a value**

For something that is not an element (an API result, a computed value), `expect.poll` retries a function:

```ts
await expect.poll(async () => {
  const res = await page.request.get('/api/shifts/1042');
  return (await res.json()).status;
}).toBe('filled');
```

This keeps you from writing your own sleep loop.

exercises.prompt:
This test is flaky. Rewrite it without a fixed sleep and add a soft assertion for the pharmacy name:

```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Cancel shift' }).click();
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
await expect(page.getByText('Cancelled')).toBeVisible();
```

exercises.answer:
```ts
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Cancel shift' }).click();
await expect(page.getByText('Cancelled')).toBeVisible();
await expect.soft(page.getByText('Maple Street Pharmacy')).toBeVisible();
```

The sleep is gone: the assertion retries until "Cancelled" appears, which is as fast as the app allows and fails with a clear message if it never does. The soft assertion records a missing pharmacy name without hiding the status check.

lessons.body:
Playwright has dozens of web-first assertions. A handful cover most tests. This drill uses the TodoMVC demo (https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/) with two todos, Buy milk and Book a flight. Every line below was run.

**Text**

```ts
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-title')).toHaveText(['Buy milk', 'Book a flight']);  // exact, in order
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-title')).toContainText(['milk', 'flight']);          // partial, in order
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-count')).toHaveText('2 items left');
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-count')).toHaveText(/2 items? left/);              // regex for 1 item / 2 items
```

`toHaveText` is a full match (after whitespace is normalised). With one todo "Book a flight", `toHaveText('Book')` fails, and the report shows `Expected: "Book"  Received: "Book a flight"`. Use `toContainText` when part of the text is the point.

**State of a control**

```ts
await expect(input).toBeEmpty();
await expect(input).toBeFocused();
await expect(checkbox).toBeChecked();
await expect(checkbox).not.toBeChecked();
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Clear completed' })).toBeHidden();
```

`toBeHidden` passes when the element is missing or not visible. `not.toBeVisible()` behaves the same way; pick one style and keep it.

**Count, attribute, class, page**

```ts
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-item')).toHaveCount(2);
await expect(page.getByRole('link', { name: 'All' })).toHaveAttribute('href', '#/');
await expect(page.getByRole('link', { name: 'All' })).toHaveClass('selected');   // whole class string
await expect(page).toHaveTitle('React • TodoMVC');
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/#\/active/);
```

`toHaveClass('selected')` needs the whole class attribute to equal the string. When other classes may be present, use a regex: `toHaveClass(/completed/)`.

**Assertion hygiene**

- One reason to fail per assertion; name the test for the behaviour.
- Assert what the user sees, not that you clicked.
- A generic `expect(x).toBe(y)` (not web-first) does not retry. Use it for plain values only, like a status code.

exercises.answer:
```ts
await expect(page).toHaveURL(/#\/active/);
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-item')).toHaveCount(1);
await expect(page.getByTestId('todo-count')).toHaveText('1 item left');
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Clear completed' })).toBeVisible();
```
I used toHaveText for the count because the whole string is the requirement; toContainText would also pass for '11 items left'.
